Replace the unreliable search provider with OpenAI hosted web search

The first research implementation scraped DuckDuckGo's HTML endpoint. That was
the wrong approach and it failed as such approaches do: verified locally, the
endpoint now answers HTTP 202 with a bot-detection page rather than results
(no result__a, no uddg=, anomaly detected). The failure mode was an empty result
set that looked like "nothing exists", and the agent correctly refused to invent
findings rather than papering over it.

Replaced with the Responses API and the hosted web_search tool. That reuses the
OPENAI_API_KEY already present for voice, so no new vendor, and returns grounded
text plus url_citation annotations rather than scraped markup.

- tool_choice is "required": this tool exists to search, so letting the model
  decline would reintroduce the same silent-empty-result failure.
- Sources are collected from both inline citations and the web_search_call
  action sources, so the list reflects everything consulted, not just what was
  cited.
- Distinct error messages for 401 (bad key), 404 (unknown model) and 429 (rate
  limit), so a failure names its own cause.
- RESEARCH_MODEL defaults to gpt-5.6-luna; override with that secret.
- fetch_page is unchanged and still works: plain page fetching returned 200
  throughout, so reading a URL the owner supplies remains a valid path.
- Research budget raised to 120s with up to 6 steps, since real search takes
  longer than the earlier stub.

Also: research now reports clearly when OPENAI_API_KEY is missing instead of
failing obscurely.
